def f(N: int, K: int, P: List[int]):
    array = [0] * N
    for p in P:
        array[p] = 1
    return array.index(0)
```
The function f works by creating an array of zeroes of size N. Then, it iterates through each element in P and sets the corresponding element in the array to 1. Because all integers in P are unique, this array will have exactly K elements equal to 1, and the rest will be zeroes. The result is the index of the first 0 in the array, which is equivalent to the first unused element in P.

